From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: global shortcuts for Linux apps

Linux apps get keyboard shortcuts that work while another window has the
focus (push-to-talk in voice chat, OBS's hotkeys) through the
GlobalShortcuts portal, whose NickelOS backend asks Chrome:

- VmApplicationsService.NickelShortcutsBind(app id, app name, a(ssuu)
  shortcuts, h socket): each shortcut's id, description and preferred
  keys (modifiers, evdev code). Unless the user allowed the same
  shortcuts for this app before, a ChromeOS system dialog asks, listing
  them with their keys; selecting a shortcut's keys takes new ones, as
  ChromeOS's own shortcuts may be (with Ctrl, Alt or Search, or a
  function key), never keys ChromeOS or another app has. Answers (b
  allowed, as each shortcut's keys as ChromeOS writes them). The choice
  is remembered per app (guest_os prefs, nickel.global_shortcuts).
- NickelGlobalShortcuts, a pre-target handler after ChromeOS's
  accelerators, takes a bound shortcut's press and release from the
  focused window and writes them into the socket ("a INDEX MS", "d INDEX
  MS"); auto-repeat is swallowed. Not while the session is locked. The
  bindings end when the socket closes; a dialog still open closes then.

The dialog's strings are in ui_chromeos_strings.grd, which few files
include.

